  "afd": {
    "available": true,
    "issued_at": "2026-10-15T11:07:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "3c75a918-9020-4a5b-8b06-d190fca614b8",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 151107\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n407 AM PDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Gusty north-northwesterly winds expected this afternoon through \n  Friday with gusts 40 to 50 mph across portions of the region.\n\n* Precipitation chances return to the region Tuesday and\n  Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Thursday.\n\nSatellite imagery early this morning showed a broad upper level \ntrough draped across the Rockies. Meanwhile to the northwest, a \nshortwave was digging south on the backside of this main trough. \nThese two pieces of energy are expected to interact to bring gusty \nnorth to northeast winds to the region today through Friday. No \nsignificant changes to previous forecast thinking. Widespread gusts \nover 40 MPH are likely over Death Valley, southeast California, and \nwestern Arizona as a 60-70KT jet sets up across the region. \nContinuing to see indications of downslope wind enhancement in \nvalleys next to terrain, including areas like Pahrump and around the \nHualapai Mountains. Impacts to valley roadways, including Interstate \n40 east of the Hualapai Mountains in southern Mohave County and \nHighway 160 in Pahrump, due to strong crosswinds are possible. No \nchanges were made to the Wind Advisories as they capture the highest \nchances for wind impacts as well as the strongest wind timing. Winds \nwill diminish through the day Friday as the trough pulls away, and \nwhile wind impacts should end by midday as gusts drop below 40 MPH- \ncontinued breezy northeast to east winds gusting 25-35 MPH are \nexpected through the afternoon. This downward trend will continue \nFriday night and Saturday, with light winds returning to the \nforecast Sunday onward. After a brief warm up to near normal today \nand Friday, temperatures drop a few degrees back to slightly below \nnormal levels for the weekend. \n\nAfter dry conditions through at least the weekend, will need to \nwatch how a deep West Coast trough interacts with moisture that may \nadvect into the region from Hurricane Simon. At the moment, moisture \ndoes not look to be as high as what we saw with the remnants of \nRachel, but what impacts we will see is uncertain as the GFS and \nECMWF have very different ideas on IVT plume locations and how they \ninteract with the incoming trough. No major changes to the \nprecipitation forecast for Tuesday and Wednesday with widespread 30%-\n40% chances that then linger into Thursday, and snow level around \n9000ft. Confidence in these details remain low though as this system \nis far into the forecast period, will continue to monitor as things \ndevelop. With the incoming system and the potential for rain, \ntemperatures will cool and remain below normal early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package... \nNortherly breezes ramp up throughout the morning, gradually turning \nmore north-northeast by the afternoon. Brief periods of wind \ndirection from 040-060 are possible, if not likely, but on average \nwind direction in the afternoon and evening should be more 010-030. \nGusts of 20-30 knots are likely (70% or more) from 15z through 03z. \nAfterwards, wind magnitude becomes more uncertain with ~50% chance \nof 20+ knot gusts overnight. VFR conditions under clear skies.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Gusty north-northeast \nwinds expected across most of the area today, with the exception \nbeing our far northwestern locations. Gusts peak in the late morning \nand afternoon with 20-35 knots likely (70% or more). Winds are \nexpected to remain elevated overnight, but just how elevated is \nuncertain. If the low levels can stay mixed out, gusts of 20-35 \nknots will be possible through the night. VFR conditions prevail \nwith clear skies throughout the TAF period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Nickerson\nAVIATION...Woods\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
